From f7d7e7ad5b4d34aa8d6b237f4fd2fc09ff23a7fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Penev <alexander.p.penev@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 03:34:57 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] core: six native HEP node types (Tube, Cone,
 SphericalShell, Trapezoid, Polyhedron, Frame)

A Geant4 detector geometry is made almost entirely of tubes, cones,
trapezoids and polyhedra placed by arbitrary rotations. Converted into a
scene it had to arrive as CustomExpr leaves, which work on every compute
path - a CustomExpr compiles to LLVM IR and to GLSL like any other node -
but CustomExprNode does not override aabb() and so inherits
AABB::infinite(). A converted detector is one object whose geometry is the
whole tree, so ONE expression leaf makes the whole scene's box infinite,
and core/accel/bvh.hpp, core/exec/rtx_executor.hpp and marching_cubes all
read that box.

The six types are 1:1 with their G4 solids, parameter for parameter and in
the same order, so a conversion is a parameter copy rather than a
translation - and a converter that mirrors its source can be checked
against that source.

  Tube            G4Tubs        rmin rmax hz phi0 dphi
  Cone            G4Cons        rmin1 rmax1 rmin2 rmax2 hz phi0 dphi
  SphericalShell  G4Sphere      rmin rmax phi0 dphi theta0 dtheta
  Trapezoid       G4Trd         dx1 dx2 dy1 dy2 hz
  Polyhedron      G4Polyhedra   rmin1 rmax1 rmin2 rmax2 hz phi0 dphi nside
  Frame           a rigid 3x4   r0..r8 tx ty tz

A polycone needs no node: it is a union of Cone sections, which is what
both Geant4 and the converter already make it.

Frame exists because three successive single-axis rotations cannot
reproduce one stored 3x3 to the bit - the angles come out of an atan2/asin
extraction and each rotation rounds again. Measured at 3.41e-13 mm, in
double, before float enters. It also collapses up to four nodes into one,
and a detector has one placement per volume.

Numerical detail that took three iterations to get right: every
trigonometric value and every slanted-face divisor sqrt(1+k^2) is computed
in DOUBLE and narrowed once. The parameters are float, and that narrowing
is a loss the export owns, but computing sin/cos or the divisor IN float
adds a second, avoidable one. With them in float the nodes disagreed with
the reference implementation by up to 2.44e-04 on a hexagonal section and
1.22e-04 on a tapered one; in double they agree bit for bit.

Verified against the G4-FRep converter (test/llvm/fd4_native_check.cc
there, built when FD4_DIR points here): on nine primitives across
prims.gdml, polyhedra_world.gdml and calib_world.gdml - hollow tube,
hollow cone, sphere with phi and theta cuts, trapezoid, polycone sections,
polyhedra - the native node's float eval() is BIT-IDENTICAL to the
CustomExpr it replaces, which is itself certified against that project's
double field. On models with no rotated frame the two whole graphs are
bit-identical. Every scene box is finite and contains the solid.

node_to_json is generic (type_name plus the whole params map), so only the
read side of scene_io needed the six branches.

